schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: Is Web3 Right for Your Community?
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: guide
  summary: >-
    A short decision-framing guide arguing web3 isn't for everyone: it lists
    when web3 adds value (transparent/verifiable finance, programmable
    governance, cross-border coordination, connecting to the wider web3
    ecosystem, immutable records) versus when it doesn't (small high-trust
    groups, minimal financial activity, low tech comfort, "a spreadsheet
    does everything you need"), five questions to ask before adopting it,
    and recommends a hybrid approach — web3 where it adds clear value,
    traditional tools elsewhere.
  audience: community groups deciding whether to adopt web3 tools at all
  known_tensions:
    - >-
      web3-genuinely-solving-a-coordination-problem-vs-adopting-it-out-of-curiosity-or-hype
  related_concepts:
    - hybrid-web3-adoption-selective-tool-use-by-function
  related_resources: []
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/is-web3-right.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
  notes: >-
    assessed-but-excluded: no resource/source-system objects were created —
    the article names no specific tool, platform, or protocol, only generic
    categories ("Web3 tools," "spreadsheets," "group chats"). See
    accompanying signal noting the article's frontmatter sources field is a
    placeholder ("Community building") rather than an actual citation.
